3. “What I’m modeling” versus “what I’m doing”

That distinction deserves its own axis.

Suppose you use a strong frame in conversation. There are at least four interpretations:

ACTION
I am trying to cause X.
EXPERIMENT
I am testing whether X happens.
MODEL
I am demonstrating the structure of X.
PERFORMANCE
I am making the structure perceptible by inhabiting it.

Same observable behavior. Completely different intent.

That is why reading behavior backward into motive is dangerous.

Pantheon should preserve:

Behavior \not\Rightarrow unique\ Intent

Instead:

Behavior \rightarrow \{Intent_1, Intent_2,\ldots Intent_n\}

then evidence updates the possibility vector.

That’s very much the thing you’ve been teaching through play: sometimes the performance is itself the diagram.

